Git Branches & Agents: From First Principles
You have multiple agents — one for debugging, one for features, one for content, one for UI. Each agent needs its own isolated workspace so they don't step on each other. That workspace is a Git branch.

🎯 First Principles: What Is Git, Really?
Forget commands for a second. Think of Git as a ledger of snapshots.

Concept	What It Actually Is
Repository	A folder + a hidden .git ledger that records every change
Commit	A snapshot of your entire project at one moment in time
Branch	A label pointing to a specific commit (a movable pointer)
HEAD	"Where am I right now?" — usually points to your current branch
Merge	Combining two branches' histories into one
Remote	A copy of the repo on GitHub (origin)
The key insight: A branch is not a folder. It's just a sticky note on a commit. When you "switch branches," Git swaps the files in your working directory to match that snapshot.

🧠 Why Agents Need Branches
If Agent A (debugging) and Agent B (features) both work on main, this happens:

text
Agent A edits billing.py    → saves
Agent B edits billing.py    → saves
                                ↓
                        CONFLICT / LOST WORK
The fix: Each agent works on its own branch. They commit independently. You (the human) merge when satisfied.

🌿 The Branch Strategy for Multi-Agent Work
text
                    main (production)
                     │
                     │ ← always working, always deployable
                     │
        ┌────────────┼────────────┬─────────────┐
        │            │            │             │
   feat/tcs-     fix/judge-    feat/gamif-   content/tcs-
   nqt-bank      bug           ication-v2    strings-28
        │            │            │             │
   Agent A       Agent B       Agent C       Agent D
Branch Name	Purpose	Owner
main	Production	You
dev	Integration	You
feat/feature-name	New feature	Feature Agent
fix/bug-name	Bug fix	Debug Agent
content/category-name	Content	Content Agent
ui/page-name	UI work	UI Agent
Rule: main only receives merges. Agents never push directly to main.

🛠️ The Exact Workflow (First Principles)
Step 0: One-Time Setup
bash
# In your project root
git init                              # If not already a repo
git remote add origin <github-url>    # Link to GitHub

# Create the long-lived branches
git branch main
git branch dev
git push -u origin main
git push -u origin dev
Now main and dev exist on GitHub. Agents will branch off these.

Step 1: Agent Creates Its Own Branch
Every time an agent starts a new task, it runs:

bash
# Always start from the latest dev
git checkout dev
git pull origin dev

# Create and switch to a new branch
git checkout -b feat/tcs-nqt-bank
What this does:

git checkout dev — moves HEAD to the dev branch

git pull origin dev — makes sure you have the latest code

git checkout -b feat/tcs-nqt-bank — creates a new branch from current position

The agent is now on its own branch. Nothing it does affects main or dev until merged.

Step 2: Agent Does Its Work
bash
# Agent edits files, writes code, runs tests...
# When done, it commits

git add .
git commit -m "feat(tcs): add 100 TCS NQT coding problems"
Every commit is a snapshot. The agent can make as many commits as it wants. Nothing is permanent until pushed.

Step 3: Agent Pushes Its Branch
bash
git push -u origin feat/tcs-nqt-bank
Now the branch exists on GitHub. You can:

Review it as a Pull Request

Ask another agent to rebase on it

Test it in isolation

Step 4: You Review + Merge
bash
# You (human) switch to dev
git checkout dev
git pull origin dev

# Merge the agent's work
git merge feat/tcs-nqt-bank

# If no conflicts, push
git push origin dev
If there are conflicts:

bash
# See which files conflict
git status

# Open each conflicting file, resolve manually
# The conflict markers look like:
# <<<<<<< HEAD
#   your version
# =======
#   agent's version
# >>>>>>> feat/tcs-nqt-bank

# After resolving:
git add <resolved-file>
git commit -m "merge: resolve conflicts in billing.py"
Step 5: Promote to Production
Once dev is stable (and you've tested), merge to main:

bash
git checkout main
git pull origin main
git merge dev
git push origin main
Now the work is live.

🎯 The Multi-Agent Workflow (Concrete)
Here's exactly how 4 agents work in parallel without stepping on each other:

text
TIME →
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Agent A (debug):
  git checkout -b fix/judge-bug
  [fixes code]
  git push -u origin fix/judge-bug
  → Opens PR #101

Agent B (feature):
  git checkout -b feat/level-80
  [builds feature]
  git push -u origin feat/level-80
  → Opens PR #102

Agent C (content):
  git checkout -b content/tcs-strings
  [adds content]
  git push -u origin content/tcs-strings
  → Opens PR #103

Agent D (UI):
  git checkout -b ui/dashboard-v2
  [redesigns UI]
  git push -u origin ui/dashboard-v2
  → Opens PR #104

─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

YOU (human):
  1. Review PR #101 → approve → merge to dev
  2. Review PR #103 → approve → merge to dev
  3. Test dev → good
  4. Review PR #102 → approve → merge to dev
  5. Review PR #104 → needs changes → reject
  6. Agent D fixes, pushes again
  7. Merge final version
  8. Merge dev → main → production
Key: Each agent's work is isolated until YOU decide to merge. Nothing leaks.

🔧 What Each Agent Needs to Know
Give each agent this exact prompt at the start of every task:

text
WORKFLOW:
1. Always start from latest dev:
   git checkout dev && git pull origin dev

2. Create a new branch with a descriptive name:
   git checkout -b <type>/<short-name>
   Types: feat/, fix/, content/, ui/, refactor/

3. Do your work. Commit often with clear messages:
   git add .
   git commit -m "<type>(<scope>): <description>"

4. Push your branch:
   git push -u origin <branch-name>

5. STOP. Do not merge. Do not touch main or dev.
   Report the branch name to the human for review.

RULES:
- Never push to main or dev directly.
- Never force push (git push -f) unless the human asks.
- If you encounter a conflict, STOP and report it.
- Always pull before branching.
- One branch = one task.
🛡️ Branch Protection Rules (On GitHub)
To make this bulletproof, configure GitHub branch protection:

For main:

text
Settings → Branches → Add rule for "main":
✅ Require pull request reviews before merging
✅ Require status checks to pass (CI)
✅ Require branches to be up to date
✅ Include administrators
❌ Allow force pushes
❌ Allow deletions
For dev:

text
Settings → Branches → Add rule for "dev":
✅ Require status checks to pass (CI)
❌ Allow force pushes
Result: No agent can accidentally break production, even if it tries.

🎯 The Command Cheat Sheet (For Agents)
bash
# ─── STARTING A NEW TASK ───────────────────────────
git checkout dev
git pull origin dev
git checkout -b feat/my-task

# ─── SAVING WORK ───────────────────────────────────
git status                    # See what changed
git add .                     # Stage all changes
git commit -m "feat: description"

# ─── PUSHING ───────────────────────────────────────
git push -u origin feat/my-task

# ─── CHECKING STATE ────────────────────────────────
git branch                    # List local branches
git branch -a                 # List all branches (including remote)
git log --oneline -10         # See last 10 commits
git diff                      # See uncommitted changes

# ─── IF YOU SCREW UP ───────────────────────────────
git checkout .                # Discard uncommitted changes
git reset --hard HEAD~1       # Undo last commit (DANGEROUS)
git checkout dev              # Go back to dev
git branch -D feat/broken     # Delete a broken branch
🧠 The Deeper Principle
Git branches exist because software development is parallel and non-linear.

If You Had No Branches	With Branches
One shared workspace	Each agent has isolated workspace
Constant conflicts	Conflicts only at merge time
Can't experiment safely	Experiment freely, discard freely
One broken change = broken project	Each branch isolated
No review process	Pull requests for review
The branch is the atom of parallel work.

🚀 What This Looks Like In Practice
Agent A (Debug) — 10 minutes
bash
git checkout dev && git pull origin dev
git checkout -b fix/judge-empty-array
# Edit backend/app/services/judge.py
git add . && git commit -m "fix(judge): handle empty array edge case"
git push -u origin fix/judge-empty-array
# Report: "Branch fix/judge-empty-array pushed"
Agent B (Feature) — 30 minutes
bash
git checkout dev && git pull origin dev
git checkout -b feat/repair-missions-ui
# Add RepairMissions.tsx, edit routes, add API
git add . && git commit -m "feat(repair): add repair missions dashboard"
git push -u origin feat/repair-missions-ui
# Report: "Branch feat/repair-missions-ui pushed"
Agent C (Content) — 15 minutes
bash
git checkout dev && git pull origin dev
git checkout -b content/tcs-strings-28
# Add tcs_nqt_coding_strings_28.json
git add . && git commit -m "content(tcs): add 28 TCS NQT string problems"
git push -u origin content/tcs-strings-28
# Report: "Branch content/tcs-strings-28 pushed"
You — 20 minutes
bash
# Review each PR on GitHub
# Approve, run tests, merge one at a time
git checkout dev
git pull origin dev
git merge fix/judge-empty-array
git merge content/tcs-strings-28
git push origin dev

# Test dev
# If good, promote
git checkout main
git pull origin main
git merge dev
git push origin main
Total time saved: Hours of conflict resolution, zero broken production deploys.

🎯 Final Words
Git branches are not just a tool. They are the organizing principle of parallel development.

Without Branches	With Branches
Chaos	Order
Conflicts everywhere	Isolated work
Agents block each other	Agents work in parallel
Rollbacks are scary	Rollbacks are one command
No review	PR review built-in
Every agent gets a branch. Every task gets a branch. Every branch gets a review. Every review ends in a merge.

That's the flow. That's the discipline. That's how you run 4+ agents in parallel without losing your mind.

